 "C13": "Press 1: Is Dosa on the menu? Say it. (Room: Say True or False.) Press 2: No Dosa today. False. (Room: Say True or False for the next one.) Press 3: Tea is there, so not in is False. (Room: Say True or False for the next one.) Press 4: A list works the same. (Room: Say True or False for the next one.) Press 5: In a string, in looks for the letters together: y, b, l side by side. (Room: Listen.)",
 "T1": "Press 1: Worksheet tab and playground. Type it, type your prediction, then run. (Room: Type, predict, run.) Press 2: You cannot change it. Make a new one, like the refund. (Room: Edit, run.) Press 3: Run it. Then make it a tuple. (Room: Run, fix, run.) Press 4: Who predicted it would print the new payment? Fair. The row is locked. And 50 is not in it: 500 is. Press 5: A new tuple with the same name. The old one was never edited. Press 6: The comma makes the tuple. tip = 10, works too.",
 "G1": "Press 1: Read it out. Say how a round works: you answer first, then it runs. (Room: Listen.) Press 2: Read all four. Option A, hands up. B. C. D. (Room: Vote with hands.) Press 3: Same as the others, or not? Say it before I press. Press 4: Same as the others, or not? Say it before I press. Press 5: Same as the others, or not? Say it before I press. Press 6: Same as the others, or not? Say it before I press. Press 7: Read the why line. What made it different? Press 8: Read all four. Option A, hands up. B. C. D. (Room: Vote with hands.) Press 9: Same as the others, or not? Say it before I press. Press 10: Same as the others, or not? Say it before I press. Press 11: Same as the others, or not? Say it before I press. Press 12: Same as the others, or not? Say it before I press. Press 13: Read the why line. What made it different? Press 14: Read all four. Option A, hands up. B. C. D. (Room: Vote with hands.) Press 15: Same as the others, or not? Say it before I press. Press 16: Same as the others, or not? Say it before I press. Press 17: Same as the others, or not? Say it before I press. Press 18: Same as the others, or not? Say it before I press. Press 19: Read the why line. What made it different?",
 "H2": "Press 1: The payment is a tuple. We want three names: shop, amount, time. How do we take them out? (Room: Say a way.) Press 2: One line per detail. It works. Press 3: How many lines now? 6. Can Python do it in one? (Room: Say yes or no.) Press 4: Three names on the left, one tuple on the right. The next card names it.",
 "C14": "That one line has a name.",
 "C15": "Press 1: One order: Maggi, 2 plates, 60 rupees. Three names, three values. Say the three lines. (Room: Say the output.) Press 2: First value, first name. That is unpacking. Press 3: Any sequence. Say what the list version prints, then the string version. (Room: Say both.) Press 4: The cart gives its first item. Teja's UPI handle ybl gives one letter per name. What if the counts do not match? Next card.",
 "E1": "Press 1: Hands up per choice. (Room: Hands up per choice.) Press 2: Who said Maggi and 2? Fair guess. Python never put Maggi anywhere. It counted first. Press 3: Four names now. Say it. (Room: Say what happens.) Press 4: Not tip left empty, not tip set to None. Nothing at all. Press 5: And three? (Room: Say it.) Press 6: Count, then fill. Left to right.",
 "G2": "Press 1: Read it out. Say how a round works: you answer first, then it runs. (Room: Listen.) Press 2: Read the left side and the right side. Option A, hands up. B. C. (Room: Vote with hands.) Press 3: Count first. Do the numbers match? Press 4: Count first. Do the numbers match? Press 5: Count first. Do the numbers match? Press 6: Count first. Do the numbers match? Press 7: Count first. Do the numbers match? Press 8: Which count decided it? Press 9: Read the left side and the right side. Option A, hands up. B. C. (Room: Vote with hands.) Press 10: Count first. Do the numbers match? Press 11: Count first. Do the numbers match? Press 12: Which count decided it? Press 13: Read the left side and the right side. Option A, hands up. B. C. (Room: Vote with hands.) Press 14: Count first. Do the numbers match? Press 15: Count first. Do the numbers match? Press 16: Count first. Do the numbers match? Press 17: Count first. Do the numbers match? Press 18: Which count decided it?",
 "C16": "Press 1: Hands up per choice. (Room: Hands up per choice.) Press 2: The commas pack it. That is why the comma, not the bracket, makes a tuple. Press 3: Teja orders 3 samosas for 45. Say the three lines. (Room: Say the output.) Press 4: Two moves in one line: pack, then unpack.",
 "C17": "Press 1: Each friend pays 16, and 2 rupees are left over. The function returns two things. One name catches it. Hands up per choice. (Room: Hands up per choice.) Press 2: return packed them into one tuple. Press 3: Say it. (Room: Say the output.) Press 4: That is how one function gives back two values. Task 2 needs it.",
 "T2": "Press 1: Two answers from one function. Which card showed us how? (Room: Say: return two values.) Press 2: Worksheet and playground. Type it, write the two lines, predict, run. (Room: Type, write, predict, run.) Press 3: This is how I wrote it in my notes. Let them react first. (Room: Change, run.) Press 4: Pack on return, unpack on the call. Press 5: The function works for any week. Press 6: One name caught the whole tuple. And count was never made.",
 "X1": "Press 1: Everyone answers alone. Then option A, hands up. B. C. D. (Room: Hands up per option.) Press 2: Go through every option, not only the right one.",
 "K1": "Press 1: Read the code. Everyone, say what it prints. Then press. (Room: Say the output.) Press 2: Read the code. Everyone, say what it prints. Then press. (Room: Say the output.) Press 3: Read the code. Everyone, say what it prints. Then press. (Room: Say the output.) Press 4: Read the code. Everyone, say what it prints. Then press. (Room: Say the output.) Press 5: Read the code. Everyone, say what it prints. Then press. (Room: Say the output.) Press 6: Read the code. Everyone, say what it prints. Then press. (Room: Say the output.)"
